[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m let out_len = bin_len * 2 + 1;
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     68[0m:[38;5;10m[49m let out: &mut [u8] = slice::from_raw_parts_mut(hex.cast::<u8>(), out_len);
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The C guard accepts `bin_len` up to `9223372036854775806`, for which
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `bin_len * 2 + 1` is `18446744073709551613` — larger than `isize::MAX`. A Rust
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m slice cannot be that long, so `from_raw_parts_mut` tripped its
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `debug_assert` and the Rust `.so` **panicked** where the C `.so` proceeded into
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     75[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the loop and faulted. The same slice-based approach also could not express
